Package: mos-board-cx3576
Version: @VERSION@
Architecture: @ARCH@
Maintainer: mos build <mos@example.invalid>
Section: admin
Priority: optional
Depends: mos-system (= @VERSION@), iproute2, kmod, mawk | awk
Description: mos board support -- CX3576-Z (Rockchip RK3576)
 Everything one cx3576 root filesystem carries that another board's does not:
 /etc/fstab, /etc/fw_env.config and /etc/rauc/system.conf rendered from
 boards/cx3576/board.env, the U-Boot environment repart definitions, the
 hardware-init programs and units with their board facts under /etc/mos, the
 status indicator, the declared AIC8800D80 firmware and the RK3576 kernel
 modules.
 .
 It also carries the board's boot inputs -- the kernel Image, the device tree,
 the U-Boot image and boot.cmd -- under /usr/lib/mos/board/cx3576. Nothing
 there is executed on the device: the image finalizer exports them from the
 installed package into the boot slots and the loader region, so which blobs
 an image was assembled from follows from its package set rather than from the
 directory the build was run beside.
 .
 /etc/mos/bt.conf ships here while hwinit-bt and mos-bt.service do not. The
 UART, the protocol and the firmware node of the Bluetooth part are facts about
 this board; the program and the unit that read them are generic and belong to
 mos-bluetooth. A board with a different radio therefore ships a different conf
 and the same application package.
 .
 There is deliberately no dependency on mos-wifi, mos-wifi-ap or mos-bluetooth.
 The cx3576 package manifest selects all three by name, and a board package
 that depended on them would take that choice away from the manifest -- a
 future board could then no longer pick client, AP and Bluetooth
 independently.
 .
 The three further dependencies are what this payload's own programs run:
 iproute2 for the ip(8) calls in hwinit-can and hwinit-mac, kmod for the
 modprobe in hwinit-gadget and hwinit-modules and for anything that loads the
 modules shipped here, and an awk for the address derivation in hwinit-mac.
 There is no ${shlibs:Depends}: the programs are shell, and the kernel modules
 are relocatable ELF whose dependencies are other modules and a kernel version
 rather than shared libraries.
